{"check": {"imports": [], "kind": "asserts", "reference": "import math\ndef wind_chill(v,t):\n windchill = 13.12 + 0.6215*t -  11.37*math.pow(v, 0.16) + 0.3965*t*math.pow(v, 0.16)\n return int(round(windchill, 0))", "tests": ["assert wind_chill(120,35)==40", "assert wind_chill(40,20)==19", "assert wind_chill(10,8)==6"]}, "id": "w9-mbpp-638", "prompt": "Write the Python function described below. You may run code to check it. End with the complete Python code in a fenced block.\n\nWrite a function to calculate the wind chill index rounded to the next integer given the wind velocity in km/h and a temperature in celsius.\nYour code should pass this test:\nassert wind_chill(120,35)==40", "semantic_sha256": "35db2fe1d9cec1558d99064435261768e67ef978feae7241e2f1d362647ddf12", "set": "mbpp", "source_lineage": "sanitized-mbpp/task_id/638", "source_prompt": "Write a function to calculate the wind chill index rounded to the next integer given the wind velocity in km/h and a temperature in celsius."}
{"check": {"imports": [], "kind": "asserts", "reference": "def count_Pairs(arr,n): \n    cnt = 0; \n    for i in range(n): \n        for j in range(i + 1,n): \n            if (arr[i] != arr[j]): \n                cnt += 1; \n    return cnt; ", "tests": ["assert count_Pairs([1,2,1],3) == 2", "assert count_Pairs([1,1,1,1],4) == 0", "assert count_Pairs([1,2,3,4,5],5) == 10"]}, "id": "w9-mbpp-564", "prompt": "Write the Python function described below. You may run code to check it. End with the complete Python code in a fenced block.\n\nWrite a python function which takes a list of integers and counts the number of possible unordered pairs where both elements are unequal.\nYour code should pass this test:\nassert count_Pairs([1,2,1],3) == 2", "semantic_sha256": "4d8af2f7019bddf4352e0e643623fcbb8c6765812ed94b48aa0c540f7b89cbb5", "set": "mbpp", "source_lineage": "sanitized-mbpp/task_id/564", "source_prompt": "Write a python function which takes a list of integers and counts the number of possible unordered pairs where both elements are unequal."}
{"check": {"imports": [], "kind": "asserts", "reference": "def count_no_of_ways(n, k): \n\tdp = [0] * (n + 1) \n\ttotal = k \n\tmod = 1000000007\n\tdp[1] = k \n\tdp[2] = k * k\t \n\tfor i in range(3,n+1): \n\t\tdp[i] = ((k - 1) * (dp[i - 1] + dp[i - 2])) % mod \n\treturn dp[n]", "tests": ["assert count_no_of_ways(2, 4) == 16", "assert count_no_of_ways(3, 2) == 6", "assert count_no_of_ways(4, 4) == 228"]}, "id": "w9-mbpp-291", "prompt": "Write the Python function described below. You may run code to check it. End with the complete Python code in a fenced block.\n\nWrite a function to find out the number of ways of painting the fence such that at most 2 adjacent posts have the same color for the given fence with n posts and k colors.\nYour code should pass this test:\nassert count_no_of_ways(2, 4) == 16", "semantic_sha256": "741b35b378bdf550b0cc2b478951dac2ca1d886f02742a709b0b035f5459b412", "set": "mbpp", "source_lineage": "sanitized-mbpp/task_id/291", "source_prompt": "Write a function to find out the number of ways of painting the fence such that at most 2 adjacent posts have the same color for the given fence with n posts and k colors."}
